  34 tools available

── create_project
   Use this when the user wants an app, site, page or tool hosted at a public web address. A project comes with a URL anyone can open without an account, sign-in for the app's own users, a dedicated database, and scheduled jobs and email that keep running afterwards. Do NOT create a project to answer a question about code, to show a snippet or an example, to explain how something works, or to edit code the user pasted.
   args: name, description

── get_project
   Get project details including slug, status, deployed functions, and the database schema (tables, columns, types).
   args: project_id

── list_agent_tasks
   Read the agent tasks the project's owner has filed — plain-English asks like "make the header say X" or "the contact form is broken", written in the console and waiting for you.
   args: project_id, status, limit

── update_agent_task
   Set the status of an agent task and leave a note on it. Both are stored on the task and shown in the project's Hatchable console, where the person who filed it reads them; nothing is sent outside Hatchable.
   args: project_id, task_id, status, note

── list_projects
   List all projects you own or collaborate on, with their tier, role, and current version.
   args: (none)

── deploy
   Deploy, publish, and host the project — put the app LIVE online at a real public URL, with a PostgreSQL database, auth, and hosting all included. Reach for this whenever the user wants to ship it, host it, put it online, make it live, publish it, launch it, or get a shareable link for the app they built. Runs migrations/*.sql (tracked so each runs once), runs seed.sql on first deploy, copies public/ files to the CDN, and registers api/ files as live endpoints. Increments the project version.
   args: project_id, intent, summary, resolves

── write_file
   Write or overwrite a project file. Paths are relative to the project root.
   args: project_id, path, content, encoding, reason

── write_files
   Write multiple project files in a single call. Same rules as write_file but batched — faster for scaffolding a new project or updating several files at once.
   args: project_id, reason, encoding, files

── read_file
   Read the content of a project file.
   args: project_id, path, offset, limit

── grep
   Regex content search across a project's files. Postgres-backed, scoped to one project, with glob filtering.
   args: project_id, pattern, glob, output_mode, -i, -n, context, head_limit

── list_files
   List all files in a project with their paths, sizes, and hashes.
   args: project_id

── patch_file
   Apply a targeted edit to an existing project file without rewriting the entire file. Finds the first occurrence of `old_string` and replaces it with `new_string`. Use this instead of write_file when modifying large files (e.g. HTML) — you only send the changed portion, not the whole file.
   args: project_id, path, old_string, new_string, reason

── delete_file
   Delete a project file. Takes effect after the next deploy.
   args: project_id, path, reason

── execute_sql
   Run SQL against the project's dedicated PostgreSQL database.
   args: project_id, sql, params

── get_schema
   Return the database schema for the project's PostgreSQL database: tables, columns (with types), and indexes.
   args: project_id

── run_function
   Execute a deployed function and return the real response. Use this to test your API endpoints.
   args: project_id, path, method, body, headers, query, as

── view_logs
   View function execution logs with rich filtering. Each entry includes status_code, duration_ms, log_output (captured console.log), error (if any), and a derived `level` field (error/warning/info).
   args: project_id, function_name, route, method, status_code, level, since, until, query, request_id, limit

── list_deployments
   List deployments for a project in reverse-chronological order. Each entry includes version, status, deployed_at, description, and summary counts (files, functions).
   args: project_id, limit

── list_functions
   List every deployed API function for a project: route, method, runtime tier, type ('scheduled' if the function has at least one active scheduled task, else 'api'), and 24-hour invocation and error counts.
   args: project_id

── get_deployment
   Detail view of one deployment by version number — returns the full file manifest (paths, hashes, sizes) and function list captured when that version shipped. Use it with list_deployments to audit or compare what changed between versions.
   args: project_id, version

── list_cron_jobs
   List a project's scheduled tasks: every recurring task, every one-shot still to fire, and the 20 most recent one-shots that already fired (older fired one-shots are counted in completed_one_shots, not listed). A task points at a function and carries a cron expression (recurring) or a one-shot fire_at, plus an optional payload delivered as the request body. Tasks are either 'declared' (written into source via export const schedule or hatchable.toml, reconciled on deploy) or 'armed' (inserted by the SDK scheduler.at() call, preserved across deploys). Response includes next_fire_at, last_fired_at, attempts, last_error, and 7-day run and 5xx-error counts from FunctionLog. Diagnostic: if next_fire_at keeps moving forward but last_fired_at never advances, the scheduler isn't running.
   args: project_id

── update_project
   Update project metadata: name, tagline, description, category. Only the fields you pass are touched. Slug and tier are immutable from MCP.
   args: project_id, name, tagline, description, category

── import_file_from_url
   Fetch a remote URL and save the response body as a project file — server-side, so the bytes never pass through your context window. Useful for seed data, vendor libs, and asset migration.
   args: project_id, url, path

── search_documentation
   Search Hatchable's own documentation for platform behavior — routing, the SDK surface, deploy semantics, auth config, runtime limits. Call this instead of guessing when you're unsure how a Hatchable feature works.
   args: query, limit

── dry_run_deploy
   Run every deploy-time validator against the project's current files without actually deploying. Returns `errors` (hard gates) and `warnings` (soft lints), plus a `would_deploy` summary of what would ship.
   args: project_id

── create_preview_link
   Mint a short-lived (30 min) preview URL showing the project exactly as an anonymous visitor would see it. On personal (private) projects it bypasses ONLY the platform sign-in wall — the app's own [auth] login and declared route access still apply, and even a signed-in owner opening it is treated as a stranger.
   args: project_id

── upload_file
   Multipart file upload for content that exceeds a single model response's output token cap (big SPA bundles, large seed data, inline vendor libs) — and the right tool for BINARY files like images, fonts, and zips: pass encoding="base64" and send the base64 text in chunks (base64 is ~33% larger than the raw bytes, so even a modest image is easier to chunk here than to inline in one write_file call).
   args: project_id, path, chunk, chunk_index, upload_id, final, sha256, bytes, encoding

── list_pending_uploads
   Show multipart uploads currently staged for this project that haven't yet been committed. Use this to recover from a disconnect — find the upload_id and resume from the next chunk_index. Uploads expire 10 minutes after the last chunk was added.
   args: project_id

── run_code
   Execute arbitrary JS in the project's isolate runtime. The SDK is pre-imported into local scope — `db`, `auth`, `admin`, `email`, `storage`, `scheduler`, `ai`, `knowledge`, `browser`, `config`, `env`, `api`, `webhooks` are ready to use without import. `process.env` and global `fetch` also work. `return` to produce the `result` field. Top-level `import` and dynamic `import('hatchable')` are NOT supported in this REPL — the bindings above are how you reach the SDK.
   args: project_id, code, timeout_ms

── fork_project
   Fork a public project into your account. Copies all code and database schema (no data). The fork starts as a personal project you can modify freely.
   args: project_id, name

── search_projects
   Search the public Hatchable project directory — other people's projects that you can view or fork. Use this to find existing apps to fork-and-modify as a starting point.
   args: query, category, limit

── list_skills
   List the registry of platform skills — discrete how-to guides for one specific task each (e.g. 'gate-an-endpoint', 'add-a-cron-job', 'add-rag-search'). Each entry is a name, one-line purpose, and category. Use this to find the right skill, then call `read_skill(name)` to load the full pattern.
   args: query, tag, model

── read_skill
   Load the full markdown body of one skill: when to use it, the canonical code shape, common pitfalls, and how to verify it works. Skills are the platform's primary agent-facing reference — every pattern an agent might need is one of these.
   args: name, model

── submit_platform_feedback
   Tell the Hatchable team about a problem with the Hatchable platform itself (a footgun, friction point, or surprising behavior) that you hit during this build. Each report is stored in Hatchable's own feedback queue, where the Hatchable team reviews it; it is not sent to any other service.
   args: severity, area, title, body, what_worked, project_id, agent_model
